hong kong international airport is a  quiet place these days  except here the check-in area for  flights to london  these people are not going away for  business or pleasure though  they're leaving for good feeling a  mixture of anger  guilt and a sense they're being forced  out this is our home  why do we need to leave i mean i have  been living here for more than 30 years  already and it's my home  so it's really sad to leave the home and  you see that everything is  deteriorating she's a finance worker  and has no job to go to like others  who've sacrificed careers  often for the sake of their children we  live here  for many years and we have  our career we have our families  so it's  a bit upset but for the  for the future of my children i think we  need to do it  these sad scenes are now being played  out daily  as more people join a growing exodus  from a territory  where communist party rule is being  tightened  in the past year an estimated 36 000  people  have left for britain under a special  visa scheme  many supported or were part of the  anti-government protest movement  like darcus hugh now in birmingham with  his family  he says the change to the immigration  law will encourage more people to go  i think the hong kong government speeds  up the immigration plan for many hong  kongers  for us we did not plan to leave hong  kong at the beginning even after the  protests in 2019.  like many others he says he's unlikely  to return to hong kong  a city that still regards itself as  asia's finest  the government denies the law gives it  unlimited powers  to stop people entering or leaving hong  kong  insisting the amendment is aimed at  screening illegal immigration at source  amid a backlog of asylum claims and that  people's right to free movement  will not be affected those who've gone  to the uk  have opted for a new life in a country  where covid 19 rates are soaring  and job prospects scarce yet that seems  preferable  to remaining here adrian brown al  jazeera  hong kong
 

